<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The one-of-a-kind records: how mood behaves, what a break is, how fast a colonist walks, what
  it starts life with, and the handful of numbers that belong to the pawn simulation as a whole.
-->
<Defs>

  <!--
    Mood is a difficulty base plus the sum of active thought offsets, approached by drift. The
    drift is what makes mood read as a mood: snapping to the target turns a single bad moment
    into an instant mental break, which is worse drama and worse design.
  -->
  <MoodDef>
    <defName>Mood_Default</defName>
    <baseMood>500</baseMood>
    <max>1000</max>
    <risePerInterval>7</risePerInterval>
    <fallPerInterval>5</fallPerInterval>
    <breakThreshold>350</breakThreshold>
    <breakMtbTicks>600000</breakMtbTicks>
  </MoodDef>

  <!-- The one break behaviour the slice carries. The taxonomy is a later milestone. -->
  <MentalBreakDef>
    <defName>Break_Wander</defName>
    <durationTicks>7500</durationTicks>
    <wanderRadius>6</wanderRadius>
    <catharsisThought>0</catharsisThought>
  </MentalBreakDef>

  <!--
    One unit of cost is 1/100 of a flat orthogonal cell crossing, so movePerTick 1 is a hundred
    ticks a cell: at sixty ticks a second that is a 2.5 m cell every 1.67 s, or 1.5 m/s — the top
    of the range a person walks at, brisk and clearly a walk.

    It used to be 2, which is 3 m/s: a jog, and on screen it was one, because the drawn walk cycle
    covers about 2 m/s and anything faster blends the run clip in. Before that it was 10, roughly
    54 km/h, and colonists visibly teleported around the map. A pace between these integers wants
    the cost scale raised, not a fraction stored.
  -->
  <MovementDef>
    <defName>Movement_Colonist</defName>
    <movePerTick>1</movePerTick>
    <!--
      The band a colonist's innate pace rolls in (WS3, design 17 §4b): rolled once per colonist
      from her seed and her id, so a seed deals the same walkers every load. INVENTED: the two
      integers are ours, and +-15 per cent is bounded by the drawing, not by taste — the walk
      cycle covers about 2 m/s and movePerTick 1 is 1.5 m/s, so anything past roughly 1,333
      would visibly jog while ostensibly walking. Everything faster is reserved for a run, and the
      first thing worth running for is a draft: draftedPacePerMille below.
    -->
    <innatePaceMinPerMille>850</innatePaceMinPerMille>
    <innatePaceMaxPerMille>1150</innatePaceMaxPerMille>
    <layerChangeEstimate>300</layerChangeEstimate>
    <!--
      A drafted colonist runs (design 33 section 2h; owner, 2026-09-23: "when you are drafted you
      should walk faster/run as this would make sense with the urgency"). Per mille of her own
      pace: 2,000 is about 3 m/s at the standard pace, past the walk cycle's 2 m/s, so the gait
      blend draws the run clip in - design 17 section 4f's run, given its first reason.
    -->
    <draftedPacePerMille>2000</draftedPacePerMille>
    <!--
      Jumping a one-cell stream (design 46 section 6). A well, unladen person's jump falls short
      30 times in a thousand and lands in the water; carrying doubles that, and a colonist in a bad
      way (hungry, exhausted, cold) is likelier still, by the inverse of the condition both rates
      already read. INVENTED. A short jump costs a soaking and a few seconds, nothing more, until
      the health model can carry an injury.
    -->
    <jumpFailPerMille>30</jumpFailPerMille>
    <jumpFailCarryingPerMille>2000</jumpFailCarryingPerMille>
  </MovementDef>

  <PawnKindDef>
    <defName>PawnKind_Colonist</defName>
    <!-- The one person species. Kind 0 must be a person; PawnContent.FromDefs refuses otherwise. -->
    <species>Species_Person</species>
    <startingMood>600</startingMood>
    <startingNeeds>
      <li>800</li>
      <li>800</li>
      <li>800</li>
    </startingNeeds>
    <wakeThreshold>950</wakeThreshold>
    <restGainPerInterval>6</restGainPerInterval>
    <groundRestEffectiveness>80</groundRestEffectiveness>
    <!--
      Recreation gained per interval while idling. The slice has no recreation buildings, so idle
      time is the only source there is; without one, joy bottoms out on every colonist and a long
      run measures nothing but mental breaks.
    -->
    <joyGainPerInterval>8</joyGainPerInterval>
    <!--
      Starvation severity per needs interval (WS3, design 17 §4c): gained while the food need is
      at zero, lost at the same rate while it is not, so one meal arrests the bar rather than
      merely stopping it. INVENTED: the design pins the three offsets, the floor and the demand
      that recovery be symmetric, and nothing about the bar's speed.

      The cadence is 400 intervals a day — a 60,000-tick day over needsIntervalTicks 150 — so one
      of a thousand fills the bar in two and a half days of an empty pantry. With the runway at
      about seventy-two hours, the first band arrives around hour eighty-seven and the worst in
      the fifth day of not eating. It was 2 against a comment that had read the cadence as 200 a
      day and so promised a bar four times slower than the one it got.
    -->
    <starvationPerInterval>1</starvationPerInterval>
    <!--
      Odds, per cent, that a freshly spawned colonist has a minor or a major passion for any one
      skill; the rest are none. ASSUMED: nothing in docs/research has measured how the reference
      distributes passions at generation. 35 and 15 give a colony of five, over two skills, a
      handful of passions and one or two burning ones.
    -->
    <passionMinorPerCent>35</passionMinorPerCent>
    <passionMajorPerCent>15</passionMajorPerCent>
    <!--
      Starting skill levels (U37), one independent roll per skill. INVENTED: there is no
      RimWorld number to take (clean room) and nothing in docs/research or docs/design pins one.
      Index is the level, value its weight out of the table's sum (100): weighted toward a low
      baseline — a colonist's life before the crash was mostly not this particular trade — with a
      thin tail so an occasional colonist starts competent rather than every one of five arriving
      identical. Mean level 1.16; levels 6 and 7 combined are a 2% roll.
    -->
    <startingSkillLevelWeights>
      <li>40</li>
      <li>20</li>
      <li>14</li>
      <li>10</li>
      <li>6</li>
      <li>4</li>
      <li>3</li>
      <li>2</li>
      <li>1</li>
    </startingSkillLevelWeights>
  </PawnKindDef>

  <!--
    dayTicks is 60,000 per a-15-time-and-simulation.md: the skill soft cap resets on it and the
    decay ladder is written per day. The calendar proper is later content; this is the one number
    the pawn simulation needs of it.

    needsIntervalTicks 150 is the cadence a-01-pawns.md measured.

    What a felled tree yields is the species' own now, in World/WildPlants.xml (design 45).

    The think-loop numbers are the trap that catches a pawn starting jobs it cannot finish: more
    than thinkLoopLimit job starts inside thinkLoopWindowTicks and it stands still for
    standDownTicks rather than spinning.
  -->
  <PawnTuningDef>
    <defName>Tuning_Pawns</defName>
    <needsIntervalTicks>150</needsIntervalTicks>
    <dayTicks>60000</dayTicks>
    <thinkLoopLimit>10</thinkLoopLimit>
    <thinkLoopWindowTicks>60</thinkLoopWindowTicks>
    <standDownTicks>120</standDownTicks>

    <!--
      What mining leaves. Both yields are ASSUMED, like the felling numbers beside them.

      stoneChanceOneIn is 1, meaning every rock cell yields. It was 4, and 4 was tuned against
      the wrong denominator: the played board holds eighty thousand cells of rock, so a yield
      from every one would put six hundred thousand stone on the map - true, and irrelevant,
      because nobody mines a board. A player mines what they mark, which is tens of cells, and at
      one in four a dozen orders produced three piles of stone against five hundred and
      sixty-seven wood from the trees beside them. The colony read as getting nothing out of the
      rock, which is what a playtest said in as many words (owner, 2026-09-16).

      It is kept as a dial rather than deleted because the machinery behind it is worth having:
      the roll is a pure function of world seed and cell index, so a partial yield can be
      reintroduced the day something wants one, without reopening how it is decided.

      Ore is never rolled: a seam cell always yields, and what it yields is the ore's own row in
      World/Ores.xml (design 62 §5c), not a number here.
    -->
    <stonePerRock>8</stonePerRock>
    <stoneChanceOneIn>1</stoneChanceOneIn>

    <!--
      How long a colonist takes to pick something up off the ground, and how far into that the
      thing changes hands. 48 ticks is 0.8 s at sixty a second, which is what the drawn gesture
      (Gesture.Lift) has always taken; until 2026-09-17 the simulation spent none of it, so the
      figure was still straightening as its pawn set off walking. Owner: "there should be time
      spent motion down, picking up object and standing up".

      The grasp is the middle of the gesture's hold, so the pile shrinks while the hands are on
      it rather than as the colonist begins to bend or after it is upright again.
    -->
    <liftTicks>48</liftTicks>
    <liftGraspTicks>24</liftGraspTicks>

    <!--
      A drafted colonist with no order and nothing to fight goes back to work after this long:
      10,000 ticks, four in-game hours, the reference's figure (a-10). Counted from the draft or
      the last order (design 33 section 2b).
    -->
    <draftQuietTicks>10000</draftQuietTicks>
  </PawnTuningDef>

</Defs>
